# bSim

bSim is a cinematic Kerr black hole renderer for Apple Silicon, built with Zig 0.16.0 and Metal.

## Features

- bSim traces past-directed light rays around a Kerr black hole with adaptive Runge-Kutta-Fehlberg integration.
- bSim renders a thin warm disk with volumetric light transport.
- bSim provides three prepared shots: a wide shot, an oblique orbit, and a close disk pass.
- The preview window supports playback, pause, restart, scrubbing, fullscreen, and scene reload.
- Exports are native 3840 by 2160 stills and animations in OpenEXR, PNG, ProRes 422 HQ, and H.264 MP4.
- The thermal guard pauses work at the temperature limits and has no bypass flag.

## Requirements

- bSim needs a Mac with Apple Silicon. The target machine is an M4 MacBook Air with 16 GB memory.
- Install the Xcode command line tools for Apple Clang.
- Install Zig 0.16.0 and ZLS 0.16.0.
- Install Nix. Bootstrap needs a dotfiles checkout at `$HOME/dotfiles`.
- Install FFmpeg and FFprobe in the global Nix profile.
- Install the just command runner and Python 3.

## Quick Start

Run these commands in the repository root.

```sh
sh scripts/bootstrap.sh
just build
just test
```

The bootstrap script checks every requirement. It also builds the thermal monitor and sets the Git hooks.
The build and test recipes run with `scripts/thermal-run.py`. This script enforces the thermal guard during long jobs.

The build uses the global Nix profile at `~/.local/state/dotfiles/nix-profile`. This profile provides OpenEXR, Imath, and FFmpeg.

## Commands

Use these commands after the build.

```sh
./zig-out/bin/bSim doctor
./zig-out/bin/bSim shots
./zig-out/bin/bSim prepare --shot wide
./zig-out/bin/bSim render --shot close --format exr
./zig-out/bin/bSim render --shot orbit --format prores --fps 24
```

The shot names are `wide`, `orbit`, and `close`. The format names are `png`, `exr`, `prores`, and `h264`.

